# ...except the artifacts the deployed service reads. Without them the Space starts and answers
# nothing, so they are part of the program, not "data". Same argument experiments/m1/.gitignore
# makes for captions and detections: regenerating these costs a 4 GB nuScenes download plus
# CPU-hours, so shipping them is what makes the deploy reproducible by someone who has neither.
# Produced by scripts/promote_artifacts.py — see experiments/M2_DESIGN.md §4.
#
# The two **vector matrices are excluded**: at 6,858 frames vectors.npy is 21 MB and
# caption_vectors.npy 10.5 MB, and docs/guide/chapter-09.md allows no committed file over 10 MB.
# float16 was measured as the obvious escape and rejected — it leaves vectors.npy at 10.5 MB, still
# over, while reordering the top-10 for 28% of queries. They live in the HF dataset instead
# (scripts/fetch_assets.py), which preserves the reproducibility argument above: a fetch is still
# "someone who has neither". What stays committed is the four small files, 4.1 MB, so the repo
# still records *what* the corpus is — only the matrices derived from it are fetched.
!data/artifacts/
data/artifacts/*
!data/artifacts/*.jsonl
!data/artifacts/*.json
data/artifacts/*.npy

# Thumbnails are NOT committed. They were, at 404 frames and 5.4 MB; at 6,858 they are 85 MB against
# a repo of ~50 MB, and the corpus only grows from here.
#
# They live in the Hugging Face dataset `P-057-RisingStar/scenesearch-assets` (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, per
# nuScenes — see docs/reference/assets-dataset-card.md) and the Dockerfile pulls them at build time, so the
# running service still serves them from local disk with nothing fetched per request.
#
# This is what M2_DESIGN §4 asked for all along: the coordinate is the fact, the pixels are derived.
# `frames.jsonl` carries video_id + timestamp_s, so a thumbnail is a cache and a cache does not
# belong in git. Repopulate locally with scripts/fetch_assets.py (or just `make run`).
data/thumbs/

